WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.050
Part 7 of nine: Use evidence to improve access.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.360
The standards connected with this part are 9, 10, 11.

3
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:12.590
Here is the work this part helps you prepare: Set

4
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.170
a practical aim and choose a process, experience and outcome

5
00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:16.570
measure

6
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.520
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 9 is described as:

7
00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.080
Goals, policies and management accountability in operations.

8
00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:30.430
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 10 is described as:

9
00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.370
Ongoing assessment and quality improvement.

10
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.180
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 11 is described as:

11
00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:41.380
Accurate, reliable demographic information used responsibly.

12
00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:43.810
Decide what improvement means.

13
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.320
Once a change is proposed, how do we decide what

14
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.520
improvement would actually mean?

15
00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:52.150
You have a proposed change and a clearer picture of

16
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:54.990
who needs to be involved. Now ask how you will

17
00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:58.490
know whether it helps. Standards nine, ten and eleven connect

18
00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:03.170
goals, accountability, ongoing assessment and reliable demographic information to improvement.

19
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:07.640
Begin with a specific aim. For the service-planning notice, an

20
00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.720
aim might be to make the next step and assistance

21
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.100
options easier to understand. That is more useful than a

22
00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:16.380
general promise to improve communication, because it points toward something

23
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:16.960
you can examine.

24
00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.930
The aim should fit your role and the available evidence.

25
00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.550
You may be preparing a recommendation rather than leading an

26
00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:29.170
evaluation. In that case, identify what information the responsible team

27
00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:33.150
would need. Do not collect personal information or create a

28
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.270
new dataset just to complete this lesson.

29
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.040
Look at process, experience and outcome.

30
00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.390
Could you walk through process, experience and outcome, and the

31
00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:44.870
question about unintended effects?

32
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.400
A process measure asks whether the planned action happened. Was

33
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.340
the revised notice reviewed? Were assistance requests acknowledged and completed

34
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.220
through the approved process? A process measure helps identify whether

35
00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:58.400
the change was implemented.

36
00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:03.370
An experience measure asks how people encountered the service. Could

37
00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:06.570
they understand the next step? Was the assistance usable? Could

38
00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:10.290
they raise a concern? Experience information can explain a problem

39
00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.510
that a completion count alone does not reveal.

40
00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:17.040
An outcome measure asks whether the intended result improved. Did

41
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.740
people have a better opportunity to participate in the service-planning

42
00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:23.580
conversation? Choose an outcome that is meaningful and reasonably connected

43
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.300
to the work. A high response rate does not automatically

44
00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.720
show that people understood their choices or felt respected.

45
00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.590
You may also need a balancing question: did the change

46
00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.770
create a new burden or barrier elsewhere? A shorter notice

47
00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:38.710
might be easier to scan but omit information some people

48
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:41.590
need. A new online option might help some people while

49
00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.830
leaving others without a usable alternative.

50
00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:46.240
Read differences carefully.

51
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:50.750
Our notice gets more responses. What should we check before

52
00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:51.990
explaining that difference?

53
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.160
Consider a learning example: one version of a notice receives

54
00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.200
more responses than another. That is a useful observation, but

55
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.080
it does not prove the wording caused the difference. The

56
00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:07.660
timing, recipients, contact method, assistance availability or other conditions may

57
00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:08.200
also differ.

58
00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:12.690
Ask about the denominator: how many people received each version?

59
00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:16.750
Ask what is missing: were undelivered notices or unanswered questions

60
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:19.610
recorded? Ask whether the measure means the same thing across

61
00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:22.690
the groups being compared. A small count can change substantially

62
00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.430
with only a few additional responses.

63
00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.600
Do not infer a person's language, disability, race or other

64
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:32.520
identity from a name or appearance. Standard eleven concerns accurate

65
00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:36.440
and reliable demographic data. Any collection, use or reporting must

66
00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.580
follow applicable privacy rules, approved methods and the purpose for

67
00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.360
which the data may be used. A training exercise is

68
00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:44.780
not authorization to collect it.

69
00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:47.510
Protect people while learning.

70
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.280
How do we learn from patterns while protecting the people

71
00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:51.960
behind the information?

72
00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:57.190
More detail is not always appropriate to publish. Small groups

73
00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.430
and combined characteristics can make individuals recognizable. Use the approved

74
00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.670
protections, reporting rules and review processes. When information is unavailable

75
00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:09.570
or cannot be shared safely, explain the limitation rather than

76
00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:10.410
inventing a conclusion.

77
00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:14.780
Numbers and experience can inform each other. If a pattern

78
00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:17.120
suggests that a process is harder to use for some

79
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.480
people, investigate with appropriate input from those affected and the

80
00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.540
responsible staff. Treat the difference as a prompt for inquiry,

81
00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:25.500
not a label attached to a community.

82
00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.470
Try a manageable improvement.

83
00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.040
What does a manageable improvement cycle look like when approval

84
00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:33.060
may still be needed?

85
00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:37.610
A useful improvement cycle is to identify the barrier, plan

86
00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:41.170
an authorized change, try it at an appropriate scale, review

87
00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.050
what happened and decide what to keep or adjust. The

88
00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:47.190
scale depends on the work. A required official notice may

89
00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:50.850
need formal approval before any trial; a personal drafting practice

90
00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:51.910
may be easier to change.

91
00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:56.480
Specify who will review the evidence and when. Without that

92
00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.620
step, a temporary fix can become permanent without anyone checking

93
00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.080
its effect. Document the reasoning so the next person can

94
00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:04.520
understand why the change was made.

95
00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:08.530
In the activity, compare three conclusions about a changed response

96
00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:12.210
rate. Choose the one that acknowledges the observation, checks context

97
00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:16.510
and proposes an appropriate next step. Avoid both declaring success

98
00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:19.710
without enough evidence and dismissing the information because it is

99
00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:20.030
imperfect.

100
00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.700
Add evidence to your plan.

101
00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:26.350
What would I write down now so the evidence can

102
00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:27.670
inform the next decision?

103
00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:32.020
For your example, write a practical aim, one process question,

104
00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.620
one experience question and one outcome question. Identify the information

105
00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:41.220
already available, any important limitation, the responsible role and a

106
00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:41.720
review date.

107
00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:45.770
If your role does not include data access, do not

108
00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:48.910
seek it through an informal workaround. You can still ask

109
00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:52.650
whether an approved evaluation exists or recommend the questions that

110
00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:53.330
should be considered.

111
00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.180
The next lesson addresses what happens when someone raises a

112
00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:01.560
concern and how to communicate progress honestly. Evidence is not

113
00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:04.940
only for an internal report. Used responsibly, it helps people

114
00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:07.400
understand what changed and what still needs attention.

115
00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:11.570
Let's work through the companion example: Choose more than a

116
00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:12.230
completion count

117
00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:15.960
Here is the situation. The team can report that a

118
00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.780
new notice was distributed, but does not yet know whether

119
00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:19.900
it is easier to use.

120
00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:23.390
What response would move this example forward responsibly?

121
00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:28.460
Process: was the revised notice and assistance offer used? Experience:

122
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.020
could people explain the next step and obtain support? Outcome:

123
00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:36.360
was participation more achievable? Check the available information, privacy limits

124
00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:37.740
and other possible influences.

125
00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.590
And here is why that response fits. An action is

126
00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:45.450
evidence of implementation, not proof of its effect. Combining questions

127
00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:46.750
gives a more useful picture.

128
00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:51.620
Now make your own choice. A revised notice has a

129
00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:54.840
higher response rate. Which conclusion is most defensible?

130
00:06:56.090 --> 00:06:59.270
Option 1. The revision eliminated access barriers for everyone.

131
00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:05.020
Option 2. The increase is worth examining alongside delivery, timing,

132
00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:08.500
group size and people's experience before deciding what it means.

133
00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:13.730
Option 3. The result is useless unless it proves that

134
00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:15.010
wording was the only cause.

135
00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.260
Pause the player now if you want to choose and

136
00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.760
explain your reasoning. You can use your own notes or

137
00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:23.420
the companion activity. When you're ready, resume and we'll work

138
00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:24.380
through all three responses.

139
00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:27.530
How should we think about option 1?

140
00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:33.100
A response rate alone cannot establish understanding, equitable access or

141
00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:36.060
a single cause. That claim goes beyond the evidence.

142
00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:38.490
How should we think about option 2?

143
00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:43.040
This uses the observation while checking its limits. Look at

144
00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:46.280
process, experience and outcomes, and protect personal information.

145
00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:50.730
Option 2 is the supported choice in this activity.

146
00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.640
How should we think about option 3?

147
00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:58.470
Imperfect information can still support improvement when its limitations are

148
00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:01.910
clear. Use it to guide appropriate inquiry rather than dismissing

149
00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:02.230
it.

150
00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.860
You can choose another response and compare the reasoning.

151
00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:10.750
Set a practical aim. Add one process question, one experience

152
00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:14.430
question, one outcome question, an information source and a review

153
00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:14.730
date.

154
00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:18.980
The working note from this part is: Evidence and review

155
00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:19.360
plan

156
00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:22.350
Pause the player if you want time to make that

157
00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:26.290
note. Keep it general, without names or private details. Thinking

158
00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:28.170
it through without writing is also fine.

159
00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:33.220
That completes part 7. You can return to any passage

160
00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:36.060
or take a break. The next part is Respond to

161
00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:37.340
concerns and share progress.
